{% extends 'layout/shell.twig' %}

{#
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  ONE AWARD'S NOMINATION PAGE
  design/NominationFlow.dc.html · phase §8.16
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  The DC's flow has five steps — Nominee, Award, Categories, Evidence, You. This page
  has FOUR, because the award step is the page: somebody who followed a link from the
  award's own page, a poster or a closing-soon email has already answered it, and asking
  again is the thing a per-award door exists to stop. `/nominate` is where that question
  is asked, and "Change award" goes back to it. Recorded in docs/handoff/GAPS.md.

  ── IT IS ONE FORM, AND THE STEPS ARE AN ENHANCEMENT ────────────────────────

  Every field is in the document from the first render and the whole thing posts to
  `/nominate` — the same door, with the same validator behind it. `nominate.js` hides
  the steps a person is not on and moves between them; with no JavaScript every section
  is simply visible and the form still submits. A five-screen wizard that only exists in
  JavaScript is a nomination form that does not work for the people this platform is
  most trying to reach.

  That is also why the counter is an enhancement and the rule is the server's: the live
  "12/40" is drawn by script, and a browser that never runs it still gets
  `NominationRules::SHORT_REASON` back from the server in the same words.

  ── THE WORDS COME FROM THE AWARD ───────────────────────────────────────────

  `wording.*` is `Support\AwardWording`, resolved per programme with the house words as
  the fallback. A form that says "nominee" under a heading reading *Carol Awards* is
  asking a choirmaster a question about a person. Nothing here writes a noun of its own.

  ── AND NOTHING HERE MENTIONS THE ANALYSIS ──────────────────────────────────

  §8.16: "No AI wording. Category-fit analysis runs server-side for admins and judges
  only." A nominator who is told a machine scored their reason is being graded by a
  form, and the first person that discourages is the one writing in a second language.
  `NominationCategoryFitTest` sweeps this file.
#}

{% set _w = wording %}
{% set _closing = programme.nominations_close %}

{# What goes back into a field: what they just typed, then what a share link carried,
   then nothing. ONE chain, at template scope, rather than `old.x ?? prefill.x ?? ''`
   written out at every input — thirty copies of a precedence rule is thirty chances
   to get one of them the wrong way round, and the wrong way round silently overwrites
   what somebody typed with what a link suggested. #}
{% set _v = (old|default({})) %}
{% set _p = (prefill|default({})) %}

{# The site header on tablet and desktop, the app bar on the phone. Both are in the
   document and CSS chooses, at one breakpoint, so there is no width with two chromes
   and none with neither. #}
{% block header %}{% include 'partials/site-header.twig' %}{% endblock %}

{#
  ── THE BAR DOES NOT REPEAT THE AWARD'S NAME ──────────────────────────────────
  The page's own `<h1>` is the award, and this bar is drawn only below 768 — so with
  the award's name here as well, a phone showed it TWICE on one screen, forty pixels
  apart. §0.5 forbids that outright and §22 lists it as a reject. Measured: two
  occurrences at 390, one at 768 and one at 1440.

  The bar answers "where am I and how do I get back"; the page answers "which award".
  The sub carries the closing date, which §3 requires to ADD to the title rather than
  repeat it.

  AND THIS NOTE IS OUT HERE FOR A REASON: a Twig comment inside a hash literal is a
  syntax error rather than a comment. Written between `back:` and `title:` it took the
  whole page to a 500.

  The first attempt at saying so took it to a 500 a second time, because it TYPED the
  closing brace-hash pair as an example — and those two characters end a Twig comment
  wherever they appear, including inside one. So this file names them and never spells
  them; the same rule the globe band's sweep records.
#}
{% block app_bar %}
  {% include 'partials/app-bar.twig' with {
    variant: 'child',
    back: '/awards/' ~ programme.slug,
    title: 'Nominate',
    sub: nominations_open
      ? (_closing ? 'Nominations close ' ~ (_closing|when('j M')) : 'Nominations open')
      : 'Nominations are not open'
  } %}
{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
<div class="nf">

  {% if not nominations_open %}
    {# ── CLOSED, AND SAYING SO ────────────────────────────────────────────
       200 and an explanation, not a 404 and not a bounce to the chooser. Whoever
       follows a link on the day is exactly the person owed the reason, and a 404
       reads as the award having been taken down. #}
    <section class="nf__shut ag-card">
      <h1>{{ programme.title }}</h1>
      {# ── WHY IT IS SHUT, AND ONLY IF THAT IS TRUE ────────────────────────
         "Nominations closed on 30 October" was being printed over a date thirty days
         in the FUTURE, because an edition can leave the nomination phase without the
         window having elapsed — this one had moved to voting. A closing date is not
         the same fact as a closed window, and printing one as the other is §19's
         shape on the page somebody came to nominate from.

         So the past date is only claimed when it IS past, and the phase carries the
         rest. #}
      <p class="nf__shut-why">
        {% if programme.phase.phase == 'upcoming' %}
          Nominations for this award have not opened yet{% if programme.nominations_open %},
          and are due to open on {{ programme.nominations_open|when('j F Y') }}{% endif %}.
        {% elseif _closing and _closing|when('U') < 'now'|date('U') %}
          Nominations closed on {{ _closing|when('j F Y') }}. This edition is now in its
          {{ programme.phase.label|default(programme.cycle_status) }} stage.
        {% elseif programme.phase.label|default('') %}
          This edition is in its {{ programme.phase.label }} stage, so it is not taking
          nominations at the moment.
        {% else %}
          This award is not taking nominations at the moment.
        {% endif %}
      </p>
      <div class="nf__shut-do">
        <a class="ag-btn ag-btn--primary" href="/awards/{{ programme.slug }}">See this award</a>
        <a class="ag-btn ag-btn--secondary" href="/nominate">Awards open now</a>
      </div>
    </section>

  {% else %}

    {% if error is defined and error %}
      <p class="nf__err" role="alert">{{ error }}</p>
    {% endif %}

    {% if share_expired|default(false) %}
      <p class="nf__err" role="alert">That shared nomination link has expired. You can
        still nominate — the form below is empty and ready.</p>
    {% elseif _p is not empty %}
      <p class="nf__note">Somebody shared this nomination with you. We have filled in what
        they knew about {{ _p.nominee_name|default('the nominee') }} — change anything, and
        it is submitted in your name.</p>
    {% endif %}

    {# Every number and sentence the script uses comes through these attributes from
       `NominationRules`. There is no 40, no 2 and no 3 in nominate.js: a literal there
       would be a second copy of a rule, and a form that counts to one number while the
       server refuses by another is the shape this codebase keeps paying for. #}
    {#
      ── THE PAGE HEAD, ABOVE THE CARD ──────────────────────────────────────
      `docs/redesign-ref/Nominate Page.dc.html` puts a kicker, a title and a lede on
      the ground, then the form in a white card below them. The lede's copy is the
      reference's, character for character.

      The H1 is the AWARD, because that is this page's subject — the reference is the
      generic `/nominate`, where the H1 is "Surface someone who matters". Each step's
      question is therefore an `<h2>`, which is also what the reference does and what
      keeps one `<h1>` per screen.
    #}
    <div class="nf__head">
      <p class="nf__kicker">Nominate</p>
      <h1 class="nf__title">{{ programme.title }}</h1>
      <p class="nf__intro">Ninety seconds to put a name on the continental record. Our panel
        verifies every profile before it joins the cycle.</p>
    </div>

    <form class="nf__form nf__card" method="post" action="/nominate"
          {# `data-ag-validate` beside `data-nf`: the shared validator draws the field
             messages and the summary, and `nominate.js` keeps the step logic and the
             category rules that are specific to this form. `novalidate` without one of
             them would be a form with the browser's bubble off and nothing in its
             place, which is what `FormErrorStateTest` sweeps for. #}
          enctype="multipart/form-data" data-nf novalidate data-ag-validate
          data-min-cats="{{ rules.min_categories }}"
          data-max-cats="{{ rules.max_categories }}"
          data-short-reason="{{ rules.short_reason }}">
      <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token }}">
      <input type="hidden" name="programme_id" value="{{ programme.id }}">

      {# The steps. `aria-current` moves with the step; with no script every panel is
         open and this list is a summary of what is being asked. #}
      {#
        The connector line and the tick are the reference's, and they carry different
        information from the number: the line says these four are one journey, and the
        tick says this one is behind you. A rail of four identical discs says neither.

        The tick is drawn by CSS on `[data-done]` rather than swapped into the markup,
        so the server's render — the one that arrives with no script — shows the plain
        numbers it should, and nothing here claims a step is finished before it is.
      #}
      <ol class="nf__rail" data-nf-rail aria-label="Steps">
        {% for s in ['Nominee', 'Categories', 'Evidence', 'You'] %}
          <li data-nf-dot="{{ loop.index }}"{% if loop.first %} aria-current="step"{% endif %}>
            <span class="nf__dot" data-n="{{ loop.index }}" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="nf__rail-t">{{ s }}</span>
          </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ol>

      {# ── 1 · WHO ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
      <section class="nf__step" data-nf-step="1" aria-labelledby="nf-h1">
        <h2 class="nf__h" id="nf-h1">{{ _w.who_question }}</h2>
        <p class="nf__lede">{{ _w.who_hint }}</p>

        {% if _w.accepts|length > 1 %}
          <div class="ag-chip-row" role="radiogroup" aria-label="What kind of nominee">
            {# `|filter`, not `for … if`: Twig 3 removed the inline `if` on a for-tag
               and answers it with "Unexpected token if", which reads as a typo rather
               than as a version difference. #}
            {% for k in kinds|filter(k => k.kind in _w.accepts) %}
              <label class="ag-chip nf__kind">
                <input type="radio" name="nominee_kind" value="{{ k.kind }}"
                       {{ loop.first ? 'checked' : '' }} data-nf-kind>
                {{ k.one }}
              </label>
            {% endfor %}
          </div>
        {% else %}
          {# One accepted kind: stated, not chosen. A radiogroup with one option is a
             control that cannot be operated, and it reads as something being broken. #}
          <input type="hidden" name="nominee_kind" value="{{ _w.accepts|first }}">
        {% endif %}

        {# The label and the hint BOTH change with the kind: "Full name" is wrong for a
           registered body and "Registered name" is wrong for a person. `nominate.js`
           swaps them from the chip that was pressed, and the server has the same rule —
           a person needs two words, an organisation does not. #}
        <label class="ag-label" for="nf-name" data-nf-name-label>{{ name_label }}</label>
        <div class="ag-field"><input id="nf-name" name="nominee_name" required
          value="{{ _v.nominee_name|default(_p.nominee_name|default('')) }}" autocomplete="off"></div>
        <p class="ag-hint" data-nf-name-hint>{{ name_hint }}</p>

        <label class="ag-label" for="nf-country">Country</label>
        <div class="ag-field">
          <select id="nf-country" name="country_code" required>
            <option value="">Choose a country</option>
            {% for code, r in regions %}
              <option value="{{ code }}" {{ _v.country_code|default(_p.country_code|default('')) == code ? 'selected' : '' }}>{{ r.name }}</option>
            {% endfor %}
          </select>
        </div>

        <label class="ag-label" for="nf-state">State or region</label>
        <div class="ag-field"><input id="nf-state" name="nominee_state" required
          value="{{ _v.nominee_state|default(_p.nominee_state|default('')) }}"></div>

        <label class="ag-label" for="nf-lga">City, town or LGA</label>
        <div class="ag-field"><input id="nf-lga" name="nominee_lga" required
          value="{{ _v.nominee_lga|default(_p.nominee_lga|default('')) }}"></div>

        {# TWO CONTROLS, TWO NAMES. One `<label>` over a pair is a label for the first
           of them: a screen reader announces the second as "edit text", and the
           placeholder that looks like its label is not an accessible name — it also
           disappears the moment somebody types, so the only person who ever saw it had
           not started. The visible heading names the pair, and each box carries its
           own. #}
        {#
          ── THE OPTIONAL PORTRAIT ─────────────────────────────────────────
          THIS WAS DROPPED AND IS BEING PUT BACK. `NominationController::submit()`
          has a complete handler for `nominee_photo` — validated, re-encoded, size
          capped, stored before the insert so the path lands on the row — and says in
          its own comment that a moderator reviews it and it seeds the profile avatar
          on approval. Both admin screens render it. The rebuild of this form simply
          did not carry the field across, so the handler had nothing to handle, the
          column stayed empty on every nomination taken since, and no screen anywhere
          said so. §18's shape, introduced by a redesign rather than inherited.

          The reference draws it as a 130x150 dashed slot beside the name. It is
          genuinely optional and says so, because a nominator often does not have a
          photograph of the person they are putting forward and must not feel stopped.
        #}
        <div class="nf__port">
          <label class="nf__port-slot" for="nf-photo">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"
                 stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
              <path d="M3 7h3l2-2h8l2 2h3v12H3z"/><circle cx="12" cy="13" r="3.5"/>
            </svg>
            <span data-nf-port-label>Add a photo</span>
          </label>
          <input id="nf-photo" class="nf__port-in" type="file" name="nominee_photo"
                 accept="image/png,image/jpeg,image/webp" data-nf-port>
          <p class="nf__port-note">Optional portrait</p>
        </div>

        <span class="ag-label" id="nf-contact-h">Their email or phone</span>
        <div class="ag-field"><input id="nf-contact" name="nominee_email" type="email"
          inputmode="email" enterkeyhint="next" autocapitalize="off" spellcheck="false"
          aria-label="Their email address"
          value="{{ _v.nominee_email|default(_p.nominee_email|default('')) }}" autocomplete="off"></div>
        <div class="ag-field"><input name="nominee_phone" type="tel"
          inputmode="tel" enterkeyhint="next"
          aria-label="Their phone number" placeholder="or a phone number"
          value="{{ _v.nominee_phone|default(_p.nominee_phone|default('')) }}" autocomplete="off"></div>
        <p class="ag-hint">So we can tell them. Never shown publicly.</p>
      </section>

      {# ── 2 · CATEGORIES ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
         The wire shape is `categories[<id>]` = the reason, so the id and the text it
         belongs to cannot be separated. Two parallel arrays — ids in one field,
         reasons in another — is how a reason ends up filed against the wrong category
         when a browser drops an empty value, and nothing on any screen would show it:
         every reason is somebody's real writing, just attached to the wrong choice. #}
      <section class="nf__step" data-nf-step="2" aria-labelledby="nf-h2">
        <h2 class="nf__h" id="nf-h2">Choose {{ rules.min_categories }} or {{ rules.max_categories }} categories</h2>
        <p class="nf__lede">Pick where their work fits best. Each one needs its own reason.</p>
        <p class="nf__count" data-nf-cats role="status">0 of {{ rules.max_categories }} chosen</p>

        {# Beside the boxes the reasons are typed into — not in a policy nobody opens,
           and not only next to Submit, which is four steps away from where somebody is
           writing. #}
        {% include 'partials/ai-collection-notice.twig' with { where: 'What you write about them' } %}

        <div class="nf__cats">
          {% for c in programme.categories %}
            <div class="nf__cat" data-nf-cat>
              <label class="nf__cat-head">
                <input type="checkbox" data-nf-pick value="{{ c.id }}"
                       {{ old.categories[c.id]|default('') ? 'checked' : '' }}>
                {# The tinted square the reference draws, with the category's own
                   initial in it. The tint rotates through the four house roles by
                   position, so a list of any length stays varied without anybody
                   choosing a colour per category — and `aria-hidden`, because the
                   title beside it already says what this is. #}
                <span class="nf__cat-ic ag-tint--{{ ['green','gold','neutral','live'][loop.index0 % 4] }}"
                      aria-hidden="true">{{ c.title|first|upper }}</span>
                <span class="nf__cat-t">{{ c.title }}</span>
                {% if c.description %}<span class="nf__cat-d">{{ c.description }}</span>{% endif %}
              </label>

              {#
                NOT `hidden` FROM THE SERVER. The reason box opened only when the
                script revealed it, so with scripting off a person could tick a
                category and had nowhere to write — and the server then refused the
                nomination for naming none. `syncCats()` closes the unchosen ones on
                boot, which is the same ordering the steps use: the server sends the
                working long form, the script makes it a wizard.
              #}
              <div class="nf__why" data-nf-why>
                <label class="ag-label" for="nf-why-{{ c.id }}"
                       data-nf-why-label data-nf-tpl="{{ _w.reason_question }}"
                       data-nf-cat-title="{{ c.title }}">
                  {{ _w.reason_question|replace({'{name}': 'them', '{category}': c.title}) }}
                </label>
                <textarea id="nf-why-{{ c.id }}" name="categories[{{ c.id }}]" rows="4"
                          data-nf-reason>{{ old.categories[c.id]|default('') }}</textarea>
                {# The number comes from the constant, never typed here: the counter and
                   the server's refusal have to be the same rule. #}
                <p class="nf__meter" data-nf-meter data-min="{{ rules.min_reason }}" role="status">
                  0/{{ rules.min_reason }}
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          {% else %}
            <p class="ag-hint">This award has no categories yet. You can still nominate —
              a reviewer will place them.</p>
          {% endfor %}
        </div>
      </section>

      {# ── 3 · EVIDENCE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
      <section class="nf__step" data-nf-step="3" aria-labelledby="nf-h3">
        <h2 class="nf__h" id="nf-h3">Add evidence <span class="nf__opt">Optional</span></h2>
        <p class="nf__lede">{{ _w.evidence_hint }}</p>

        <div data-nf-links>
          {% for i in 1..rules.max_evidence %}
            {# NAMED INDIVIDUALLY. One group label cannot name five boxes — a screen
               reader announces "edit text" five times, indistinguishable — and the
               placeholder is not a name: it vanishes on the first keystroke, so the
               only person who ever read it had not started typing. #}
            <div class="ag-field nf__link"{{ i > 1 ? ' hidden' : '' }}>
              <input name="evidence_links[]" type="url" inputmode="url"
                     aria-label="Evidence link {{ i }} of {{ rules.max_evidence }}"
                     placeholder="Paste a link — an article, a video, a website"
                     value="{{ _v.evidence_links[i - 1]|default('') }}">
            </div>
          {% endfor %}
        </div>
        <button type="button" class="ag-btn ag-btn--text" data-nf-add-link>Add another link</button>

        <label class="ag-label" for="nf-files">Or upload a file</label>
        <div class="ag-field">
          <input id="nf-files" name="evidence[]" type="file" multiple
                 accept=".pdf,.jpg,.jpeg,.png,application/pdf,image/jpeg,image/png">
        </div>
        <p class="ag-hint">PDF, JPG or PNG · up to {{ rules.max_file_mb }} MB each ·
          {{ rules.max_evidence }} pieces of evidence in total.</p>
      </section>

      {# ── 4 · YOU ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
      <section class="nf__step" data-nf-step="4" aria-labelledby="nf-h4">
        <h2 class="nf__h" id="nf-h4">About you</h2>
        <p class="nf__lede">Every nominator is checked, so every nomination counts.</p>

        <label class="ag-label" for="nf-you">Your full name</label>
        <div class="ag-field"><input id="nf-you" name="nominator_name" required
          enterkeyhint="next" autocomplete="name"
          value="{{ old.nominator_name|default(member.name|default('')) }}"></div>

        <label class="ag-label" for="nf-you-email">Your email</label>
        <div class="ag-field"><input id="nf-you-email" name="nominator_email" type="email" required
          inputmode="email" enterkeyhint="next" autocapitalize="off" spellcheck="false"
          autocomplete="email"
          value="{{ old.nominator_email|default(member.email|default('')) }}"></div>

        <label class="ag-label" for="nf-you-phone">Your phone</label>
        <div class="ag-field"><input id="nf-you-phone" name="nominator_phone" type="tel" required
          inputmode="tel" enterkeyhint="next" autocomplete="tel"
          value="{{ old.nominator_phone|default('') }}"></div>

        <label class="ag-label" for="nf-you-country">Your country</label>
        <div class="ag-field">
          <select id="nf-you-country" name="nominator_country" required>
            <option value="">Choose a country</option>
            {% for code, r in regions %}
              <option value="{{ code }}" {{ old.nominator_country|default('') == code ? 'selected' : '' }}>{{ r.name }}</option>
            {% endfor %}
          </select>
        </div>

        <label class="ag-label" for="nf-you-state">Your state or region</label>
        <div class="ag-field"><input id="nf-you-state" name="nominator_state" required
          value="{{ old.nominator_state|default('') }}"></div>

        <label class="ag-label" for="nf-you-lga">Your city, town or LGA</label>
        <div class="ag-field"><input id="nf-you-lga" name="nominator_lga" required
          value="{{ old.nominator_lga|default('') }}"></div>

        <label class="ag-label" for="nf-you-age">Your age range</label>
        <div class="ag-field">
          <select id="nf-you-age" name="nominator_age_range" required>
            <option value="">Choose one</option>
            {% for a in ['under 18', '18–24', '25–34', '35–44', '45–54', '55–64', '65+'] %}
              <option value="{{ a }}" {{ old.nominator_age_range|default('') == a ? 'selected' : '' }}>{{ a }}</option>
            {% endfor %}
          </select>
        </div>

        <label class="nf__consent">
          <input type="checkbox" name="consent" value="1" required
                 {{ old.consent|default('') ? 'checked' : '' }}>
          <span>I confirm this nomination is genuine, and I accept the
            <a href="/awards/{{ programme.slug }}#terms">terms of this award</a>.</span>
        </label>
      </section>

      {# The action bar is in the form, so Submit is a real submit button and Enter
         works from any field. A bar outside the form needs `form=` on every control,
         which is the sort of thing that is right on the day and wrong after a move. #}
      <div class="nf__bar" data-bottom-ui>
        {#
          ── THE SUMMARY §8 ASKS FOR ────────────────────────────────────────
          "[summary: bold value + 12.5px detail] [primary]". On a shop that is the
          price; here the thing somebody needs held in front of them is WHERE THEY
          ARE and what this step still wants — a four-step form whose bar says only
          "Continue" gives no sense of how much is left, and the step rail scrolls
          away above the fold.

          It is filled by `nominate.js` from the step it is on, so there is no second
          list of step names here. With no script it reads "Step 1 of 4", which is
          true of the page a person without JavaScript sees — every panel open, and
          this bar's Submit the one button that matters.
        #}
        <p class="nf__sum" data-nf-sum>
          <b data-nf-sum-v>Step 1 of 4</b>
          <span data-nf-sum-d>Who you are nominating</span>
        </p>
        {#
          ── THE SERVER SENDS A WORKING FORM; THE SCRIPT MAKES IT A WIZARD ─────
          THIS WAS THE WRONG WAY ROUND. Steps two to four shipped `hidden` and Submit
          shipped `hidden`, so with no JavaScript the page was step one and no way to
          send it — while this file's own stylesheet said, in as many words, that
          every section is simply visible and the form still posts. §19's shape, in a
          comment I wrote.

          Measured with scripting off: one of four panels visible, no submit button,
          twenty-nine fields that could never be sent.

          So the defaults are the no-script defaults. `Continue` is the wizard's own
          control and does nothing without it, so IT is the one that starts hidden;
          `show()` reveals it on boot and hides the panels. Everything here is then
          true of both pages — the one with the script and the one without.
        #}
        <button type="button" class="ag-btn ag-btn--secondary" data-nf-back hidden>Back</button>
        <button type="button" class="ag-btn ag-btn--primary ag-btn--block" data-nf-next hidden>Continue</button>
        <button type="submit" class="ag-btn ag-btn--primary ag-btn--block" data-nf-submit>
          Submit nomination
        </button>
      </div>

      {#
        ── THE SUBMITTING STATE ────────────────────────────────────────────
        The reference has it, and §13 makes it a MUST: a form that posts a file
        upload over a slow connection with no sign that anything happened is a form
        somebody presses twice. It covers the card rather than replacing it, so the
        answers stay visible behind it and the page does not jump.

        `hidden` until the submit handler has decided the form is going — never on
        press, because the step-2 rules can still refuse and then the person would
        watch a spinner for a submission that never left.
      #}
      <div class="nf__busy" data-nf-busy hidden role="status" aria-live="polite">
        <span class="nf__spin" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <p>Submitting nomination…</p>
      </div>
    </form>

    {# The reference's line, character for character. It answers the three questions
       somebody hesitates over at the last step — what it costs, how long it takes,
       and whether their address is about to be sold. #}
    <p class="nf__foot">Free · takes about 90 seconds · OTP-secured</p>
  {% endif %}
</div>
{% endblock %}

{% block head_styles %}
  {# This page's own sheet, and not a block in `components.css`: that file is the base
     layer every screen loads, and a `.nf__cat` in it is fifty lines the whole site
     downloads to use none of. `components/nav.css` is the cautionary tale — it held the
     vote countdown's rules under a navigation name and the redesign nearly deleted them. #}
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('/assets/css/components/nominate.css') }}">
{% endblock %}

{% block foot_scripts %}
  <script defer src="{{ asset('/assets/js/nominate.js') }}"></script>
{% endblock %}
